Whoa, easy there, don't bite the Shakespearean monkeys among us!
Thanks for pointing out that anal-retentiveness (if you can really
call it a skill) is *part* of the skill set required, along with
careful preparation, resourcefulness and ingenuity. Yes, I have made
many mistakes, but unless I was so "cocaine-laden" at the time that I
made several in quick succession, and *additionally* did not prepare
for mistakes to occur, Nethack usually provided *some* (albeit
tedious) method of escape. See my recent post in Cdi's ascension
thread for details.

For instance, several of my high-level characters acquired
teleportitis early on by munching on those juicy tengus. You know
what? It isn't the end of the world. When I was playing carefully, I
polypiled for rings of teleport control, or wished for the master key
of thievery when chaotic due to the half phys damage, or made it a
goal (as a wizard) to alchemize potions of gain level to gain
intrinsic control. Once, when I *wasn't* playing carefully, I acquired
teleportitis *and* polymorphitis by eating tengus and *then* trying on
some shiny new (unfortunately cursed) 300 zorkmid ring, in the
fleeting hope that it was teleport control. Oh goodness, turning into
a grid bug, having your armor and newly charged WoD fall around you,
teleporting away from them both, and then meeting some orgasmically
happy gnome wearing a suspiciously familiar +3 hawaiian shirt wielding
said wand against you is quite bad.

My playing style has improved because I *plan ahead for mistakes to
happen*. I no longer place items as important (and light!) as magic
markers, essential scrolls and wands, or rings in bags of holding, and
place then instead in an oilskin sack once I find one. My goal, in
fact, is to treat the bag of holding as a "mobile stash" of extra crap
I *might need* on the way up/down to my *real* stash. So when my bag
of holding gets cursed because I forgot to obsessively dip it in holy
water (stupid monkey!), it's light enough that I can still *hold it*
and dip it into holy water/cancel it/etc.

I've also improved because I have played *all* the classes and races
in an attempt to understand the utility of common dungeon items. I
never realized how useful dust-written Elbereth was until I played a
flimsy Archaeologist on a protection run. I additionally learned to
use the pick-axe to dig traps, and to use combinations of dust-
Elbereth and digging to provide a hidy-hole safe from wands and
daggers. Once I understood how useful common tools could be, I was
much more resourceful when I made mistakes, as I had many, many more
methods of escape.

As damning as Nethack is of mistakes, part of the fun in nethack is
recovering from bad situations, and those situations may include, or
at least be induced by, mistakes. If you're trying to make the case
that *winning* nethack means never making mistakes - or worse, that
this is the central skill for beating nethack, that troubles me. I am
reminded of software designers who leave out debugging routes or
neglect to comment their code because they "don't make mistakes in the
first place".

